Gri 405: Diversity And Equal Opportunity 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 405-1 Diversity of governance bodies and employees The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Percentage of individuals within the organization’s governance bodies in each of the following diversity categories: Gender;i. Age group: under 30 years old, 30-50 years old, over 50 years old;ii. Other indicators of diversity where relevant (such as minority or vulnerable groups). iii. a. Percentage of employees per employee category in each of the following diversity categories: Gender;i. Age group: under 30 years old, 30-50 years old, over 50 years old;ii. Other indicators of diversity where relevant (such as minority or vulnerable groups). iii. b. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 405-1, the reporting organization should use data from Disclosure 2-7 in GRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 to identify the total number of employees. 2.1 Guidance for Disclosure 405-1 Examples of governance bodies that exist within an organization can be the board of directors, management committee, or a similar body for a non-corporate organization. An organization can identify any other indicators of diversity used in its own monitoring and recording that are relevant for reporting. Background This disclosure provides a quantitative measure of diversity within an organization and can be used in conjunction with sectoral or regional benchmarks. Comparisons between broad employee diversity and management team diversity offer information on equal opportunity. Information reported in this disclosure also helps in assessing which issues can be of particular relevance to certain segments of the governance bodies or employees. GUIDANCE GRI 405: Diversity and Equal Opportunity 2016 8 Disclosure 405-2 Ratio of basic salary and remuneration of women to men The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Ratio of the basic salary and remuneration of women to men for each employee category, by significant locations of operation. a. The definition used for ‘significant locations of operation’.b. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 405-2, the reporting organization should base remuneration on the average pay of each gender grouping within each employee category. 2.2 Guidance for Disclosure 405-2 The reporting organization can draw from the information used for Disclosure 405-1 to identify the total number of employees in each employee category by gender. Background An organization can take an active role in reviewing its operations and decisions, in order to promote diversity, eliminate gender bias, and support equal opportunity. These principles apply equally to recruitment, opportunities for advancement, and remuneration policies. Equality of remuneration is also an important factor in retaining qualified employees. GUIDANCE GRI 405: Diversity and Equal Opportunity 2016 9",topic disclosures disclosure diversity governance bodies employees reporting organization report following information requirements percentage individuals organization’s governance bodies each following diversity categories gender i age group under years old years old over years old other indicators diversity where relevant such minority vulnerable groups iii percentage employees per employee category each following diversity categories gender i age group under years old years old over years old other indicators diversity where relevant such minority vulnerable groups iii recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization use data disclosure gri general disclosures identify total number employees guidance disclosure governance bodies exist organization can board directors management committee similar body non corporate organization organization can identify any other indicators diversity used its own monitoring recording are relevant reporting background disclosure provides quantitative measure diversity organization can used conjunction sectoral regional benchmarks comparisons between broad employee diversity management team diversity offer information equal opportunity information reported disclosure helps assessing which issues can particular relevance certain segments governance bodies employees guidance gri diversity equal opportunity disclosure ratio basic salary remuneration women men reporting organization report following information requirements ratio basic salary remuneration women men each employee category significant locations operation definition used ‘significant locations operation’ recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization base remuneration average pay each gender grouping each employee category guidance disclosure reporting organization can draw information used disclosure identify total number employees each employee category gender background organization can take active role reviewing its operations decisions order promote diversity eliminate gender bias support equal opportunity these principles apply equally recruitment opportunities advancement remuneration policies equality remuneration important factor retaining qualified employees guidance gri diversity equal opportunity,"['years old', 'reporting organization', 'organization can', 'governance bodies', 'equal opportunity']","['diversity governance', 'employee diversity', 'indicators diversity', 'diversity management', 'diversity organization']","diversity governance, employee diversity, indicators diversity, diversity management, diversity organization, years old, reporting organization, organization can, governance bodies, equal opportunity"
Gri 406: Non-discrimination 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 406-1 Incidents of discrimination and corrective actions taken The reporting organization shall report the following information: Compilation requirements REQUIREMENTS Total number of incidents of discrimination during the reporting period.a. Status of the incidents and actions taken with reference to the following: Incident reviewed by the organization;i. Remediation plans being implemented;ii. Remediation plans that have been implemented, with results reviewed through routine internal management review processes; iii. Incident no longer subject to action.iv. b. When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 406-1, the reporting organization shall include incidents of discrimination on grounds of race, color, sex, religion, political opinion, national extraction, or social origin as defined by the ILO, or other relevant forms of discrimination involving internal and/or external stakeholders across operations in the reporting period. 2.1 Guidance for Disclosure 406-1 In the context of this disclosure, an ‘incident’ refers to a legal action or complaint registered with the reporting organization or competent authorities through a formal process, or an instance of non-compliance identified by the organization through established procedures. Established procedures to identify instances of non-compliance can include management system audits, formal monitoring programs, or grievance mechanisms . An incident is no longer subject to action if it is resolved, the case is completed, or no further action is required by the organization. For example, an incident for which no further action is required can include cases that were withdrawn or where the underlying circumstances that led to the incident no longer exist. Background According to ILO instruments, discrimination can occur on the grounds of race, color, sex, religion, political opinion, national extraction, and social origin. Discrimination can also occur based on factors such as age, disability, migrant status, HIV and AIDS, gender, sexual orientation, genetic predisposition, and lifestyles, among others. The presence and effective implementation of policies to avoid discrimination are a basic expectation of responsible business conduct. GUIDANCE 1 1 Source: International Labour Organization (ILO), Report I(B) - Equality at work: The continuing challenge - Global Report under the follow-up to the ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, 2011. GRI 406: Non-discrimination 2016 8",topic disclosures disclosure incidents discrimination corrective actions taken reporting organization report following information compilation requirements requirements total number incidents discrimination during reporting period status incidents actions taken reference following incident reviewed organization i remediation plans being implemented remediation plans have been implemented results reviewed through routine internal management review processes iii incident no longer subject action iv when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization include incidents discrimination grounds race color sex religion political opinion national extraction social origin defined ilo other relevant forms discrimination involving internal external stakeholders across operations reporting period guidance disclosure context disclosure ‘incident’ refers legal action complaint registered reporting organization competent authorities through formal process instance non compliance identified organization through established procedures established procedures identify instances non compliance can include management system audits formal monitoring programs grievance mechanisms incident no longer subject action if it resolved case completed no further action required organization incident which no further action required can include cases were withdrawn where underlying circumstances led incident no longer exist background according ilo instruments discrimination can occur grounds race color sex religion political opinion national extraction social origin discrimination can occur based factors such age disability migrant status hiv aids gender sexual orientation genetic predisposition lifestyles others presence effective implementation policies avoid discrimination are basic expectation responsible business conduct guidance source international labour organization ilo report i equality work continuing challenge global report under follow up ilo declaration fundamental principles rights work gri non discrimination,"['reporting organization', 'incident no', 'no longer', 'incidents discrimination', 'social origin']","['incidents discrimination', 'discrimination reporting', 'discrimination occur', 'discrimination involving', 'discrimination 2016']","incidents discrimination, discrimination reporting, discrimination occur, discrimination involving, discrimination 2016, reporting organization, incident no, no longer, incidents discrimination, social origin"
Gri 407: Freedom Of Association And Collective Bargaining 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"1. Topic management disclosures An organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report how it manages each of its material topics . An organization that has determined freedom of association and collective bargaining to be a material topic is required to report how it manages the topic using Disclosure 3-3 in GRI 3: Material Topics 2021 (see clause 1.1 in this section). This section is therefore designed to supplement – and not replace – Disclosure 3-3 in GRI 3. REQUIREMENTS The reporting organization shall report how it manages freedom of association and collective bargaining using Disclosure 3-3 in GRI 3: Material Topics 2021. 1.1 RECOMMENDATIONS The reporting organization should describe any policy or policies considered likely to affect workers ’ decisions to form or join a trade union, to bargain collectively or to engage in trade union activities. 1.2 GRI 407: Freedom of Association and Collective Bargaining 2016 7 2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 407-1 Operations and suppliers in which the right to freedom of association and collective bargaining may be at risk The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Operations and suppliers in which workers ’ rights to exercise freedom of association or collective bargaining may be violated or at significant risk either in terms of: type of operation (such as manufacturing plant) and supplier;i. countries or geographic areas with operations and suppliers considered at risk.ii. a. Measures taken by the organization in the reporting period intended to support rights to exercise freedom of association and collective bargaining. b. Guidance for Disclosure 407-1 The process for identifying operations and suppliers, as specified in Disclosure 407-1, can reflect the reporting organization’s approach to risk assessment on this issue. It can also draw from recognized international data sources, such as the various outcomes of the ILO Supervisory bodies and the recommendations of the ILO Committee of Freedom of Association (see reference [4] in the Bibliography ). When reporting the measures taken, the organization can refer to the ILO ‘Tripartite Declaration of Principles Concerning Multinational Enterprises and Social Policy’ and Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises for further guidance. Background This disclosure concerns an organization’s due diligence with respect to any negative impacts its activities have had on the human rights of workers to form or join trade unions and to bargain collectively. This can include policies and processes with respect to the organization's business relationships, including its suppliers. It can also include the due diligence process to identify operations and suppliers where these rights are at risk. It also aims to reveal actions that have been taken to support these rights across an organization’s range of operations. This disclosure does not require the organization to express a specific opinion on the quality of national legal systems. Collective agreements can be made at the level of the organization, at the level of a particular site, at the industry level, and at the national level in countries where this is the practice. Collective agreements can cover specific groups of workers, for example, those performing a specific activity or working at a specific location. An organization is expected to respect the rights of workers to exercise freedom of association and collective bargaining. It is also expected to not benefit from or contribute to such violations through its business relationships (e.g., suppliers). GUIDANCE GRI 407: Freedom of Association and Collective Bargaining 2016 8",topic management disclosures organization reporting accordance gri standards required report how it manages each its material topics organization determined freedom association collective bargaining material topic required report how it manages topic using disclosure gri material topics clause section section therefore designed supplement not replace disclosure gri requirements reporting organization report how it manages freedom association collective bargaining using disclosure gri material topics recommendations reporting organization describe any policy policies considered likely affect workers ’ decisions form join trade union bargain collectively engage trade union activities gri freedom association collective bargaining topic disclosures disclosure operations suppliers which right freedom association collective bargaining risk reporting organization report following information requirements operations suppliers which workers ’ rights exercise freedom association collective bargaining violated significant risk either terms type operation such manufacturing plant supplier i countries geographic areas operations suppliers considered risk measures taken organization reporting period intended support rights exercise freedom association collective bargaining guidance disclosure process identifying operations suppliers specified disclosure can reflect reporting organization’s approach risk assessment issue it can draw recognized international data sources such various outcomes ilo supervisory bodies recommendations ilo committee freedom association reference bibliography when reporting measures taken organization can refer ilo ‘tripartite declaration principles concerning multinational enterprises social policy’ organisation economic co operation development oecd oecd guidelines multinational enterprises further guidance background disclosure concerns organization’s due diligence respect any negative impacts its activities have had human rights workers form join trade unions bargain collectively can include policies processes respect organization s business relationships including its suppliers it can include due diligence process identify operations suppliers where these rights are risk it aims reveal actions have been taken support these rights across organization’s range operations disclosure does not require organization express specific opinion quality national legal systems collective agreements can made level organization level particular site industry level national level countries where practice collective agreements can cover specific groups workers those performing specific activity working specific location organization expected respect rights workers exercise freedom association collective bargaining it expected not benefit contribute such violations through its business relationships suppliers guidance gri freedom association collective bargaining,"['freedom association', 'collective bargaining', 'association collective', 'operations suppliers', 'reporting organization']","['disclosure gri', 'suppliers rights', 'topic disclosures', 'disclosures organization', 'gri standards']","disclosure gri, suppliers rights, topic disclosures, disclosures organization, gri standards, freedom association, collective bargaining, association collective, operations suppliers, reporting organization"
Gri 408: Child Labor 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 408-1 Operations and suppliers at significant risk for incidents of child labor The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Operations and suppliers considered to have significant risk for incidents of: child labor;i. young workers exposed to hazardous work.ii. a. Operations and suppliers considered to have significant risk for incidents of child labor either in terms of: type of operation (such as manufacturing plant) and supplier;i. countries or geographic areas with operations and suppliers considered at risk.ii. b. Measures taken by the organization in the reporting period intended to contribute to the effective abolition of child labor. c. Guidance for Disclosure 408-1 The process for identifying operations and suppliers, as specified in Disclosure 408-1, can reflect the reporting organization’s approach to risk assessment on this issue. It can also draw from recognized international data sources, such as the ILO Information and reports on the application of Conventions and Recommendations (see reference [1] in the Bibliography ). When reporting the measures taken, the organization can refer to the ILO ‘Tripartite Declaration of Principles Concerning Multinational Enterprises and Social Policy’ and Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises for further guidance. In the context of the GRI Standards, a ‘young worker’ is defined as a person above the applicable minimum working age and younger than 18 years of age. Note that Disclosure 408-1 does not require quantitative reporting on child labor or the number of young workers. Rather, it asks for reporting on the operations and suppliers considered to have significant risk for incidents of child labor or young workers exposed to hazardous work. Background Child labor is subject to ILO Conventions 138 ‘Minimum Age Convention’ (ILO Convention 138) and 182 ‘Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention’ (ILO Convention 182). ‘Child labor’ refers to an abuse, which is not to be confused with ‘children working’ or with ‘young persons working’, which may not be abuses as stipulated in ILO Convention 138. The minimum age for working differs by country. ILO Convention 138 specifies a minimum age of 15 years or the age of completion of compulsory schooling (whichever is higher). However, there is an exception for certain countries where economies and educational facilities are insufficiently developed and a minimum age of 14 years might apply. These countries of exception are specified by the ILO in response to special application by the country concerned, and in consultation with representative organizations of employers and workers. ILO Convention 138 stipulates that ‘national laws or regulations may permit the employment or work of persons 13 to 15 years of age on light work which is (a) not likely to be harmful to their health or development; and (b) not such as to prejudice their attendance at school, their participation in vocational orientation or training programmes approved by the competent authority or their capacity to benefit from the instruction received’. While child labor takes many different forms, a priority is to eliminate without delay the worst forms of child labor as defined by Article 3 of ILO Convention 182. This includes all forms of slavery or practices similar to slavery (such as sale, trafficking, forced or compulsory labor, GUIDANCE GRI 408: Child Labor 2016 8 serfdom, recruitment for armed conflict); the use, procuring or offering of a child for prostitution or illicit activities and any work that is likely to harm the health, safety or morals of children. ILO Convention 182 is intended to set priorities for states; however, organizations are expected not to use this convention to justify forms of child labor. Child labor results in under-skilled and unhealthy workers for tomorrow and perpetuates poverty across generations, thus impeding sustainable development. The abolition of child labor is therefore necessary for both economic and human development. GRI 408: Child Labor 2016 9",topic disclosures disclosure operations suppliers significant risk incidents child labor reporting organization report following information requirements operations suppliers considered have significant risk incidents child labor i young workers exposed hazardous work operations suppliers considered have significant risk incidents child labor either terms type operation such manufacturing plant supplier i countries geographic areas operations suppliers considered risk measures taken organization reporting period intended contribute effective abolition child labor c guidance disclosure process identifying operations suppliers specified disclosure can reflect reporting organization’s approach risk assessment issue it can draw recognized international data sources such ilo information reports application conventions recommendations reference bibliography when reporting measures taken organization can refer ilo ‘tripartite declaration principles concerning multinational enterprises social policy’ organisation economic co operation development oecd oecd guidelines multinational enterprises further guidance context gri standards ‘young worker’ defined person above applicable minimum working age younger than years age note disclosure does not require quantitative reporting child labor number young workers rather it asks reporting operations suppliers considered have significant risk incidents child labor young workers exposed hazardous work background child labor subject ilo conventions ‘minimum age convention’ ilo convention ‘worst forms child labour convention’ ilo convention ‘child labor’ refers abuse which not confused ‘children working’ ‘young persons working’ which not abuses stipulated ilo convention minimum age working differs country ilo convention specifies minimum age years age completion compulsory schooling whichever higher exception certain countries where economies educational facilities are insufficiently developed minimum age years might apply these countries exception are specified ilo response special application country concerned consultation representative organizations employers workers ilo convention stipulates ‘national laws regulations permit employment work persons years age light work which not likely harmful health development not such prejudice attendance school participation vocational orientation training programmes approved competent authority capacity benefit instruction received’ while child labor takes many different forms priority eliminate delay worst forms child labor defined article ilo convention all forms slavery practices similar slavery such sale trafficking forced compulsory labor guidance gri child labor serfdom recruitment armed conflict use procuring offering child prostitution illicit activities any work likely harm health safety morals children ilo convention intended set priorities states organizations are expected not use convention justify forms child labor child labor results under skilled unhealthy workers tomorrow perpetuates poverty across generations thus impeding sustainable development abolition child labor therefore necessary both economic human development gri child labor,"['child labor', 'ilo convention', 'operations suppliers', 'minimum age', 'significant risk']","['child labour', 'child labor', 'children ilo', 'specified ilo', 'stipulated ilo']","child labour, child labor, children ilo, specified ilo, stipulated ilo, child labor, ilo convention, operations suppliers, minimum age, significant risk"
Gri 409: Forced Or Compulsory Labor 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 409-1 Operations and suppliers at significant risk for incidents of forced or compulsory labor The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Operations and suppliers considered to have significant risk for incidents of forced or compulsory labor either in terms of: type of operation (such as manufacturing plant) and supplier;i. countries or geographic areas with operations and suppliers considered at risk.ii. a. Measures taken by the organization in the reporting period intended to contribute to the elimination of all forms of forced or compulsory labor. b. Guidance for Disclosure 409-1 The process for identifying operations and suppliers, as specified in Disclosure 409-1, can reflect the reporting organization’s approach to risk assessment on this issue. It can also draw from recognized international data sources, such as the ILO Information and reports on the application of Conventions and Recommendations (see reference [1] in the Bibliography ). When reporting the measures taken, the organization can refer to the ILO ‘Tripartite Declaration of Principles Concerning Multinational Enterprises and Social Policy’ and Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises for further guidance. Background Forced or compulsory labor exists globally in a variety of forms. The most extreme examples are slave labor and bonded labor, but debts can also be used as a means of maintaining workers in a state of forced labor. Indicators of forced labor can also include withholding identity papers, requiring compulsory deposits, and compelling workers, under threat of firing, to work extra hours to which they have not previously agreed. Eliminating forced labor remains an important challenge. Forced labor is not only a serious violation of a fundamental human right, it also perpetuates poverty and is a hindrance to economic and human development. The presence and effective implementation of policies for eliminating all forms of forced or compulsory labor are a basic expectation of responsible business conduct. Organizations with multinational operations are required by law in some countries to provide information on their efforts to eradicate forced labor in their supply chains. GUIDANCE 5 5 International Labour Organization (ILO), International Labour Standards on Forced labour http://www.ilo.org/global/standards/subjects-covered-by- international-labour-standards/forced-labour/lang--en/index.htm#P23_4987, accessed on 1 September 2016. GRI 409: Forced or Compulsory Labor 2016 8",topic disclosures disclosure operations suppliers significant risk incidents forced compulsory labor reporting organization report following information requirements operations suppliers considered have significant risk incidents forced compulsory labor either terms type operation such manufacturing plant supplier i countries geographic areas operations suppliers considered risk measures taken organization reporting period intended contribute elimination all forms forced compulsory labor guidance disclosure process identifying operations suppliers specified disclosure can reflect reporting organization’s approach risk assessment issue it can draw recognized international data sources such ilo information reports application conventions recommendations reference bibliography when reporting measures taken organization can refer ilo ‘tripartite declaration principles concerning multinational enterprises social policy’ organisation economic co operation development oecd oecd guidelines multinational enterprises further guidance background forced compulsory labor exists globally variety forms most extreme are slave labor bonded labor but debts can used means maintaining workers state forced labor indicators forced labor can include withholding identity papers requiring compulsory deposits compelling workers under threat firing work extra hours which they have not previously agreed eliminating forced labor remains important challenge forced labor not only serious violation fundamental human right it perpetuates poverty hindrance economic human development presence effective implementation policies eliminating all forms forced compulsory labor are basic expectation responsible business conduct organizations multinational operations are required law some countries provide information efforts eradicate forced labor supply chains guidance international labour organization ilo international labour standards forced labour http www ilo org global standards subjects covered international labour standards forced labour lang en index htm p23 accessed september gri forced compulsory labor,"['forced compulsory', 'compulsory labor', 'forced labor', 'operations suppliers', 'international labour']","['forced labour', 'forced labor', 'compulsory labor', 'refer ilo', 'ilo org']","forced labour, forced labor, compulsory labor, refer ilo, ilo org, forced compulsory, compulsory labor, forced labor, operations suppliers, international labour"
Gri 410: Security Practices 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 410-1 Security personnel trained in human rights policies or procedures The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Percentage of security personnel who have received formal training in the organization’s human rights policies or specific procedures and their application to security. a. Whether training requirements also apply to third-party organizations providing security personnel. b. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 410-1-a, the reporting organization should: 2.1 calculate the percentage using the total number of security personnel, whether they are employees of the organization or employees of third-party organizations; 2.1.1 state whether employees of third-party organizations are also included in the calculation. 2.1.2 Guidance for Disclosure 410-1 The training can refer either to training dedicated to the subject of human rights or to a human rights module within a general training program. Training can cover issues such as the use of force, inhuman or degrading treatment or discrimination, or identification and registering. Background The use of security personnel can play an essential role in allowing an organization to operate in a safe and productive manner, and can contribute to the security of local communities and populations. However, as set out in the International Code of Conduct for Private Security Service Providers, the use of security personnel can also have negative impacts on local populations and on the upholding of human rights and the rule of law. According to the UN Human Rights Office of the High Commissioner, ‘human rights education constitutes an essential contribution to the long-term prevention of human rights abuses and represents an important investment in the endeavor to achieve a just society in which all human rights of all persons are valued and respected. Training security personnel in human rights can therefore help to ensure their appropriate conduct towards third parties, particularly regarding the use of force. This disclosure indicates the proportion of the security force that can reasonably be assumed to be aware of an organization’s expectations of human rights performance. Information provided under this disclosure can demonstrate the extent to which management systems pertaining to human rights are implemented. GUIDANCE 1 1 United Nations Human Rights Office of the High Commissioner (OHCHR), http://www.ohchr.org/EN/Issues/Education/Training/Pages/HREducationTrainingIndex.aspx , accessed on 1 September 2016. GRI 410: Security Practices 2016 8",topic disclosures disclosure security personnel trained human rights policies procedures reporting organization report following information requirements percentage security personnel who have received formal training organization’s human rights policies specific procedures application security whether training requirements apply third party organizations providing security personnel recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization calculate percentage using total number security personnel whether they are employees organization employees third party organizations state whether employees third party organizations are included calculation guidance disclosure training can refer either training dedicated subject human rights human rights module general training program training can cover issues such use force inhuman degrading treatment discrimination identification registering background use security personnel can play essential role allowing organization operate safe productive manner can contribute security local communities populations set out international code conduct private security service providers use security personnel can have negative impacts local populations upholding human rights rule law according un human rights office high commissioner ‘human rights education constitutes essential contribution long term prevention human rights abuses represents important investment endeavor achieve just society which all human rights all persons are valued respected training security personnel human rights can therefore help ensure appropriate conduct towards third parties regarding use force disclosure indicates proportion security force can reasonably assumed aware organization’s expectations human rights performance information provided under disclosure can demonstrate extent which management systems pertaining human rights are implemented guidance united nations human rights office high commissioner ohchr http www ohchr org en issues education training pages hreducationtrainingindex aspx accessed september gri security practices,"['human rights', 'security personnel', 'party organizations', 'third party', 'office high']","['training security', 'security training', 'human rights', 'rights human', 'rights education']","training security, security training, human rights, rights human, rights education, human rights, security personnel, party organizations, third party, office high"
Gri 411: Rights Of Indigenous Peoples 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 411-1 Incidents of violations involving rights of indigenous peoples The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Total number of identified incidents of violations involving the rights of indigenous peoples during the reporting period. a. Status of the incidents and actions taken with reference to the following: Incident reviewed by the organization;i. Remediation plans being implemented;ii. Remediation plans that have been implemented, with results reviewed through routine internal management review processes; iii. Incident no longer subject to action.iv. b. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 411-1, the reporting organization should include incidents involving the rights of indigenous peoples among: 2.1 workers performing the organization’s activities;2.1.1 communities likely to be affected by existing or planned activities of the organization. 2.1.2 Guidance for Disclosure 411-1 In the context of this disclosure, an ‘incident’ refers to a legal action or complaint registered with the reporting organization or competent authorities through a formal process, or an instance of non-compliance identified by the organization through established procedures. Established procedures to identify instances of non-compliance can include management system audits, formal monitoring programs, or grievance mechanisms . Background The number of recorded incidents involving the rights of indigenous peoples provides information about the implementation of an organization’s policies relating to indigenous peoples. This information helps to indicate the state of relations with stakeholder communities. This is particularly important in regions where indigenous peoples reside, or have interests near operations of the organization. GUIDANCE GRI 411: Rights of Indigenous Peoples 2016 8",topic disclosures disclosure incidents violations involving rights indigenous peoples reporting organization report following information requirements total number identified incidents violations involving rights indigenous peoples during reporting period status incidents actions taken reference following incident reviewed organization i remediation plans being implemented remediation plans have been implemented results reviewed through routine internal management review processes iii incident no longer subject action iv recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization include incidents involving rights indigenous peoples workers performing organization’s activities communities likely affected existing planned activities organization guidance disclosure context disclosure ‘incident’ refers legal action complaint registered reporting organization competent authorities through formal process instance non compliance identified organization through established procedures established procedures identify instances non compliance can include management system audits formal monitoring programs grievance mechanisms background number recorded incidents involving rights indigenous peoples provides information about implementation organization’s policies relating indigenous peoples information helps indicate state relations stakeholder communities important regions where indigenous peoples reside have interests near operations organization guidance gri rights indigenous peoples,"['indigenous peoples', 'rights indigenous', 'involving rights', 'reporting organization', 'incidents involving']","['rights indigenous', 'indigenous peoples', 'incidents violations', 'context disclosure', 'relating indigenous']","rights indigenous, indigenous peoples, incidents violations, context disclosure, relating indigenous, indigenous peoples, rights indigenous, involving rights, reporting organization, incidents involving"
Gri 413: Local Communities 2016,Gri Standards,2018-Jul,"2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 413-1 Operations with local community engagement, impact assessments, and development programs The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Percentage of operations with implemented local community engagement, impact assessments, and/or development programs, including the use of: social impact assessments, including gender impact assessments, based on participatory processes; i. environmental impact assessments and ongoing monitoring;ii. public disclosure of results of environmental and social impact assessments;iii. local community development programs based on local communities’ needs;iv. stakeholder engagement plans based on stakeholder mapping;v. broad based local community consultation committees and processes that include vulnerable groups; vi. works councils, occupational health and safety committees and other worker representation bodies to deal with impacts; vii. formal local community grievance processes.viii. a. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 413-1, the reporting organization should use the data from Disclosure 2-6 in GRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 to identify the total number of operations, if the organization has reported its total number of operations when describing its activities. 2.1 Background A key element in managing impacts on people in local communities is assessment and planning in order to understand the actual and potential impacts, and strong engagement with local communities to understand their expectations and needs. There are many elements that can be incorporated into local community engagement, impact assessments, and development programs. This disclosure seeks to identify which elements have been consistently applied, organization-wide. Where possible, organizations are expected to anticipate and avoid negative impacts on local communities. Where this is not possible, or where residual impacts remain, organizations are expected to manage those impacts appropriately, including grievances, and to compensate local communities for negative impacts. Establishing a timely and effective stakeholder identification and engagement process is important to help organizations understand the vulnerability of local communities and how these might be affected by the organization’s activities. A stakeholder engagement process both in early planning stages as well as during operations, can help establish lines of communication between an organization’s various departments (planning, finance, environment, production, etc.) and key stakeholder interest groups in the community. This enables an organization to consider the views of community stakeholders in its decisions, and to address its potential impacts on local communities in a timely manner. Organizations can utilize a number of useful tools to engage communities, including social and human rights impact assessments, which include a diverse set of approaches for proper identification of stakeholders and community characteristics. These can be based on issues such as ethnic background, indigenous descent, gender, age, migrant status, socioeconomic status, literacy levels, disabilities, income level, infrastructure availability or specific human health vulnerabilities which may exist within stakeholder communities. An organization is expected to consider the differentiated nature of local communities and to take specific action to identify and engage vulnerable groups. This might require adopting differentiated measures to allow the effective participation of vulnerable groups, such as making GUIDANCE GRI 413: Local Communities 2016 8 information available in alternate languages or format for those who are not literate or who do not have access to printed materials. Where necessary, organizations are expected to establish additional or separate processes so that negative impacts on vulnerable or disadvantaged groups are avoided, minimized, mitigated or compensated. 1 1 International Finance Corporation (IFC), Guidance Notes: Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability, 2012. GRI 413: Local Communities 2016 9 Disclosure 413-2 Operations with significant actual and potential negative impacts on local communities The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS Operations with significant actual and potential negative impacts on local communities, including: the location of the operations;i. the significant actual and potential negative impacts of operations.ii. a. RECOMMENDATIONS When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 413-2, the reporting organization should: 2.2 report the vulnerability and risk to local communities from potential negative impacts due to factors including: 2.2.1 the degree of physical or economic isolation of the local community;2.2.1.1 the level of socioeconomic development, including the degree of gender equality within the community; 2.2.1.2 the state of socioeconomic infrastructure, including health and education infrastructure; 2.2.1.3 the proximity to operations;2.2.1.4 the level of social organization;2.2.1.5 the strength and quality of the governance of local and national institutions around local communities; 2.2.1.6 report the exposure of the local community to its operations due to higher than average use of shared resources or impact on shared resources, including: 2.2.2 the use of hazardous substances that have an impact on the environment and human health in general, and specifically have an impact on reproductive health; 2.2.2.1 the volume and type of pollution released;2.2.2.2 the status as major employer in the local community;2.2.2.3 land conversion and resettlement;2.2.2.4 natural resource consumption;2.2.2.5 for each of the significant actual and potential negative economic, social, cultural, and/or environmental impacts on local communities and their rights, describe: 2.2.3 the intensity or severity of the impact;2.2.3.1 the likely duration of the impact;2.2.3.2 the reversibility of the impact;2.2.3.3 the scale of the impact.2.2.3.4 Guidance for Disclosure 413-2 Internal sources of information about actual and potential negative impacts of operations on local communities can include: Background This disclosure is focused on significant actual and potential negative impacts related to an organization’s operations and not on community investments or donations, which are addressed by GRI 201: Economic Performance 2016 . This disclosure informs stakeholders about an organization’s awareness of its negative impacts on local communities. It also enables the organization to better prioritize and improve GUIDANCE actual performance data;• internal investment plans and associated risk assessments;• all data collected with topic disclosures as they relate to individual communities. For example: GRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 , GRI 301: Materials 2016 , GRI 302: Energy 2016 , GRI 303: Water and Effluents 2018, GRI 304: Biodiversity 2016 , GRI 305: Emissions 2016 , GRI 306: Waste 2020, GRI 403: Occupational Health and Safety 2018 , GRI 408: Child Labor 2016 , GRI 409: Forced or Compulsory Labor 2016 , GRI 410: Security Practices 2016 , GRI 411: Rights of Indigenous Peoples 2016 , and GRI 416: Customer Health and Safety 2016 . • GRI 413: Local Communities 2016 10",topic disclosures disclosure operations local community engagement impact assessments development programs reporting organization report following information requirements percentage operations implemented local community engagement impact assessments development programs including use social impact assessments including gender impact assessments based participatory processes i environmental impact assessments ongoing monitoring public disclosure results environmental social impact assessments iii local community development programs based local communities’ iv stakeholder engagement plans based stakeholder mapping v broad based local community consultation committees processes include vulnerable groups vi works councils occupational health safety committees other worker representation bodies deal impacts vii formal local community grievance processes viii recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization use data disclosure gri general disclosures identify total number operations if organization reported its total number operations when describing its activities background key element managing impacts people local communities assessment planning order understand actual potential impacts strong engagement local communities understand expectations are many elements can incorporated into local community engagement impact assessments development programs disclosure seeks identify which elements have been consistently applied organization wide where possible organizations are expected anticipate avoid negative impacts local communities where not possible where residual impacts remain organizations are expected manage those impacts appropriately including grievances compensate local communities negative impacts establishing timely effective stakeholder identification engagement process important help organizations understand vulnerability local communities how these might affected organization’s activities stakeholder engagement process both early planning stages well during operations can help establish lines communication between organization’s various departments planning finance environment production key stakeholder interest groups community enables organization consider views community stakeholders its decisions address its potential impacts local communities timely manner organizations can utilize number useful tools engage communities including social human rights impact assessments which include diverse set approaches proper identification stakeholders community characteristics these can based issues such ethnic background indigenous descent gender age migrant status socioeconomic status literacy levels disabilities income level infrastructure availability specific human health vulnerabilities which exist stakeholder communities organization expected consider differentiated nature local communities take specific action identify engage vulnerable groups might require adopting differentiated measures allow effective participation vulnerable groups such making guidance gri local communities information available alternate languages format those who are not literate who do not have access printed materials where necessary organizations are expected establish additional separate processes so negative impacts vulnerable disadvantaged groups are avoided minimized mitigated compensated international finance corporation ifc guidance notes performance standards environmental social sustainability gri local communities disclosure operations significant actual potential negative impacts local communities reporting organization report following information requirements operations significant actual potential negative impacts local communities including location operations i significant actual potential negative impacts operations recommendations when compiling information specified disclosure reporting organization report vulnerability risk local communities potential negative impacts due factors including degree physical economic isolation local community level socioeconomic development including degree gender equality community state socioeconomic infrastructure including health education infrastructure proximity operations level social organization strength quality governance local national institutions around local communities report exposure local community its operations due higher than average use shared resources impact shared resources including use hazardous substances have impact environment human health general specifically have impact reproductive