Guideline B-15 Readiness Self-assessment Questionnaire: What We Heard Report,Osfi,2024-Feb,"3/20 approaches that are holistic, integrated, and built on reliable empirical data and sound analyses. It also necessitates FRFIs to continuously monitor and incorporate developments in climate risk management, such as improving data quality and evolving risk measurement methodologies, into their governance and risk management practices. In March 2023, OSFI released the Guideline B-15: Climate Risk Management /en/guidance/guidance-library/climate-risk-management), (Guideline), which provides FRFIs with principles-based guidance to manage their climate-related risks. The Guideline establishes three expected outcomes that FRFIs should achieve: 1. The FRFI understands and mitigates against potential impacts of climate-related risks to its business model and strategy 2. The FRFI has appropriate governance and risk management practices to manage identified climate-related risks 3. The FRFI remains financially resilient through severe, yet plausible, climate risk scenarios, and operationally resilient through disruption due to climate-related disasters 1.1 The Guideline B-15 readiness self-assessment questionnaire To gauge FRFI readiness to meet the Guideline expectations by the implementation timelines, we issued a Guideline B-15 readiness self-assessment questionnaire (SAQ) in May 2023. The SAQ focused on the FRFIs' progress and plans to meet the expectations per Chapter 1, which include: • Principle 1: The FRFI should have the appropriate governance and accountability structure in place to manage climate-related risks • Principle 2: The FRFI should incorporate the implications of physical risks from climate change and the risks associated with the transition to a low-greenhouse gas 2/8/24, 10:08 AM Guideline B-15 readiness self-assessment questionnaire: What we heard report - Office of the Superintendent of Financial Instituti... economy to the FRFI in its business model and strategy • Principle 3: The FRFI should manage and mitigate climate-related risks in accordance with the FRFI's Risk Appetite Framework • Principle 4: The FRFI should use climate scenario analysis to assess the impact of climate-related risks on its risk profile, business strategy, and business model • Principle 5: The FRFI should maintain sufficient capital and liquidity buffers for its climate-related risks A separate section of the questionnaire included questions about FRFIs' current readiness to report on key categories of our climate-related financial disclosures expectations per Chapter 2. We received over 210 responses from in-scope FRFIs in July 2023. The submissions included group filings for FRFIs that are owned by the same parent entity and represents coverage of over 95% of in-scope FRFIs. For this report, we grouped these FRFIs into three industry groups: • Deposit-taking institutions (DT's), including banks, and trust and loan companies • Life insurers, including fraternal benefit societies • Property and casualty insurers (P&C), including mortgage insurers This What We Heard report highlights the key themes and emerging practices based on FRFI responses to the SAQ. It does not represent an OSFI assessment of the FRFI readiness to meet the Guideline expectations. 2.0 Key themes from the SAQ responses While there were diverse FRFI responses, we identified four key themes: • FRFIs are progressing gradually to embed climate risk management into their governance structures, frameworks, and processes https://www.osfi-bsif.gc.ca/en/consultations/consultation-summaries/guideline-b-15-readiness-self-assessment-questionnaire-what-we-heard-report 4/20 5/20 • FRFIs are in the early stages of quantifying the impacts of climate-related risks • FRFIs are preparing to report on most categories of the expected climate-related financial disclosures per Chapter 2 of the Guideline • Additional guidance from OSFI could help FRFIs build capabilities to understand and manage climate-related risks Each of the four themes will be discussed in detail below. 2.1 FRFIs are progressing gradually to embed climate risk management into their governance structures, frameworks, and processes Effective climate risk management starts with clear direction from the Board of Directors (Board) and Senior Management, coupled with appropriate structures, frameworks, and processes throughout an organization to manage these risks. This involves the FRFIs having a good understanding of the climate-related risks to which they can be exposed, as well as clarity around the roles and responsibilities of those in the organization to manage them. Furthermore, it requires FRFIs to incorporate climate-related risks into their enterprise risk management (ERM) frameworks, risk appetite frameworks, and internal control frameworks (ICF). It also requires FRFIs to have processes in place to support timely and accurate reporting to monitor risks and make decisions. Based on the SAQ responses, over 50% of FRFIs across all industry groups: • formalized, or are formalizing, roles and responsibilities for the Board, Senior Management, and Branch Management • incorporated, or are incorporating, climate-related risks into their ERM frameworks and risk appetite frameworks • report, or are starting to report, climate-related risks to their Board and Senior Management",approaches are holistic integrated built reliable empirical data sound analyses it necessitates frfis continuously monitor incorporate developments climate risk management such improving data quality evolving risk measurement methodologies into governance risk management practices march osfi released guideline climate risk management en guidance guidance library climate risk management guideline which provides frfis principles based guidance manage climate related risks guideline establishes three expected outcomes frfis achieve frfi understands mitigates against potential impacts climate related risks its business model strategy frfi appropriate governance risk management practices manage identified climate related risks frfi remains financially resilient through severe yet plausible climate risk scenarios operationally resilient through disruption due climate related disasters guideline readiness self assessment questionnaire gauge frfi readiness guideline expectations implementation timelines issued guideline readiness self assessment questionnaire saq saq focused frfis progress plans expectations per which include principle frfi have appropriate governance accountability structure place manage climate related risks principle frfi incorporate implications physical risks climate change risks associated transition low greenhouse gas am guideline readiness self assessment questionnaire what heard report office superintendent financial instituti economy frfi its business model strategy principle frfi manage mitigate climate related risks accordance frfi s risk appetite framework principle frfi use climate scenario analysis assess impact climate related risks its risk profile business strategy business model principle frfi maintain sufficient capital liquidity buffers its climate related risks separate section questionnaire included questions about frfis current readiness report key categories climate related financial disclosures expectations per received over responses scope frfis july submissions included group filings frfis are owned same parent entity represents coverage over scope frfis report grouped these frfis into three industry groups deposit taking institutions dt s including banks trust loan companies life insurers including fraternal benefit societies property casualty insurers p c including mortgage insurers what heard report highlights key themes emerging practices based frfi responses saq it does not represent osfi assessment frfi readiness guideline expectations key themes saq responses while were diverse frfi responses identified four key themes frfis are progressing gradually embed climate risk management into governance structures frameworks processes https www osfi bsif gc ca en consultations consultation summaries guideline readiness self assessment questionnaire what heard report frfis are early stages quantifying impacts climate related risks frfis are preparing report most categories expected climate related financial disclosures per guideline additional guidance osfi help frfis build capabilities understand manage climate related risks each four themes will discussed detail below frfis are progressing gradually embed climate risk management into governance structures frameworks processes effective climate risk management starts clear direction board directors board senior management coupled appropriate structures frameworks processes throughout organization manage these risks involves frfis having good understanding climate related risks which they can exposed well clarity around roles responsibilities those organization manage them furthermore it frfis incorporate climate related risks into enterprise risk management erm frameworks risk appetite frameworks internal control frameworks icf it frfis have processes place support timely accurate reporting monitor risks make decisions based saq responses over frfis across all industry groups formalized are formalizing roles responsibilities board senior management branch management incorporated are incorporating climate related risks into erm frameworks risk appetite frameworks report are starting report climate related risks board senior management,"['climate related', 'related risks', 'risk management', 'climate risk', 'principle frfi']","['frfi risk', 'assessment frfi', 'risks frfis', 'risks frfi', 'frfis report']","frfi risk, assessment frfi, risks frfis, risks frfi, frfis report, climate related, related risks, risk management, climate risk, principle frfi"
